WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.560
Hello everyone, welcome to the lecture on 3D computer vision, and today we are going

00:04.560 --> 00:08.040
to talk about structure for motion and bundle adjustment.

00:08.040 --> 00:13.080
Hopefully, by the end of today's lecture, you'll be able to describe the pipeline of

00:13.080 --> 00:15.200
large-scale 3D reconstruction.

00:15.200 --> 00:20.640
It consists of three parts, namely the data association, structure for motion, and dense

00:20.640 --> 00:22.680
stereo algorithm.

00:22.680 --> 00:27.760
In today's lecture, we'll look at data association and structure for motion in more detail, and

00:27.760 --> 00:35.440
we'll look at dense stereo algorithm in more detail in next week's lecture.

00:35.440 --> 00:41.200
You should be able to explain the use of robust two-view geometry and the back-of-words algorithm

00:41.200 --> 00:48.520
for data association, and next, we'll look at how to use the two-view geometry, the PNP

00:48.520 --> 00:53.620
algorithm as well as the linear triangulation algorithm, which we have learned in the previous

00:53.620 --> 01:01.260
lectures to initialise the 3D reconstruction, and finally, we'll look at how to apply iterative

01:01.260 --> 01:06.820
methods such as Newton, Gauss-Newton, and the Levenberg-Macquois algorithm for bundle

01:06.820 --> 01:07.820
adjustment.

01:07.820 --> 01:13.580
Of course, I didn't invent any of today's material, and I took most of the materials

01:13.580 --> 01:21.860
from the tutorial on large-scale 3D modelling, which was a tutorial conducted at CVPR 2017.

01:21.860 --> 01:26.860
Here's the link to all the slides and the content of this particular lecture.

01:26.860 --> 01:32.060
I strongly encourage every one of you to look at these slides and the content after today's

01:32.060 --> 01:37.700
lecture to reinforce your idea on large-scale 3D reconstruction.

01:37.700 --> 01:42.980
And I also took a lot of the materials on bundle adjustment from Richard Hartlick's

01:42.980 --> 01:47.660
and Andrew Zisserman's textbook, Multiview Geometry in Computer Vision, in particular,

01:47.660 --> 01:49.180
Appendix 6.

01:49.180 --> 01:53.500
More information on bundle adjustment can be found in the paper that is written by Bill

01:53.500 --> 01:59.980
Trigg's bundle adjustment, A Modern Synthesis, in 1999, and if you are interested, you should

01:59.980 --> 02:06.180
look at Chapter 11 of my East textbook, An Invitation to 3D Computer Vision, to find

02:06.180 --> 02:13.980
out more about the general pipeline of 3D reconstruction as well as bundle adjustment.

02:13.980 --> 02:20.300
Now, the problem of large-scale 3D reconstruction can be cast as follows.

02:20.300 --> 02:25.620
Suppose that we are given a set of images denoted by I1 to IN.

02:25.620 --> 02:35.620
Let's say if this is the set of images where we have I1, I2, all the way to IN over here.

02:35.620 --> 02:42.100
The objective of large-scale 3D reconstruction is to first find out the motions of the cameras

02:42.100 --> 02:45.140
that have taken these particular images.

02:45.140 --> 02:51.660
This simply means that given a world reference frame, which we denote as Fw over here, what

02:51.660 --> 02:59.580
we want to first figure out would be the camera projection matrices denoted by P1, P2, as

02:59.580 --> 03:01.300
well as Pn.

03:01.300 --> 03:06.380
And we have seen that we can do this using two techniques, which is the relative post-estimation

03:06.380 --> 03:11.020
as well as the absolute post-estimation in our earlier lecture.

03:11.020 --> 03:16.940
And then once the motion of the cameras, that means that the camera projection matrices

03:16.940 --> 03:22.820
of these cameras have been found, the next thing that we want to do would be to recover

03:22.820 --> 03:28.540
the 3D structures from the image correspondences as well as the camera projection matrices

03:28.540 --> 03:32.620
triangulate for the 3D structure.

03:32.620 --> 03:37.940
For example, if I have a correspondence over here, I know P1 and P2, we have seen in the

03:37.940 --> 03:45.700
earlier lecture that we can do linear triangulation to obtain this particular 3D point over here.

03:45.700 --> 03:51.940
And today, we are going to put everything together to see how we can do this for n views.

03:51.940 --> 03:57.140
That means that if I have n images, I want to do the motion estimation of the camera

03:57.140 --> 04:03.940
as well as the linear triangulation algorithm to get all the 3D points that is seen across

04:03.940 --> 04:08.020
every single image in this collection of images.

04:08.020 --> 04:12.740
In the case where we are just interested in recovering the 3D points that corresponds

04:12.740 --> 04:17.780
to these image correspondences, then this would be known as the structure from motion,

04:17.780 --> 04:18.780
which we call SFM.

04:18.780 --> 04:24.980
In fact, the name implies that the steps over here, because we are finding the motion of

04:24.980 --> 04:33.740
the cameras first before we find the 3D structure, hence the name structure from motion.

04:34.060 --> 04:39.380
In next week's lecture, we will also look at the case where we are interested in recovering

04:39.380 --> 04:44.380
the 3D point of every single pixel in the collection of all the images.

04:44.380 --> 04:56.100
As a result, we will get a dense 3D reconstruction, and that would be called a dense stereo algorithm,

04:56.100 --> 05:01.100
which we will look at in more detail in next week's lecture.

05:01.100 --> 05:06.420
As I mentioned earlier on, there are three key parts of the pipeline for large scale

05:06.420 --> 05:08.420
3D reconstruction.

05:08.420 --> 05:14.060
The first would be to do data association, because we are given a set of images which

05:14.060 --> 05:16.880
are in unordered sequence.

05:16.880 --> 05:23.420
That means that I give you I1, I2, all the way to IN over here, but I do not know how

05:23.420 --> 05:26.240
are all these images related to each other.

05:26.240 --> 05:32.680
So the first step would be to figure out whether they are related to each other geometrically,

05:32.680 --> 05:38.680
in the sense that we want to suppose that I take any pair of the images from this collection

05:38.680 --> 05:48.640
of images over here, let's say I denote this as II and IJ over here, I want to find out

05:48.640 --> 05:53.800
whether these two images are seeing the same 3D scene or not.

05:53.800 --> 06:00.560
So the obvious thing to do here would be to first extract image correspondences, and

06:00.560 --> 06:08.160
then compute the relative transformation using the fundamental matrix or the essential matrix

06:08.160 --> 06:12.640
to see whether these two images are related.

06:12.640 --> 06:17.200
If they are related, this means that the number of image correspondences here after the robust

06:17.200 --> 06:23.360
estimation of the fundamental or essential matrix would be more than a certain threshold.

06:23.800 --> 06:28.400
The next thing that we want, once we have established the image correspondences and

06:28.400 --> 06:33.300
the data association, the next thing that we want to do would be to do the structure

06:33.300 --> 06:38.520
for motion, as I mentioned earlier on, is that given a pair of image over here, we will

06:38.520 --> 06:43.600
first compute the pose, which we have done computing the fundamental or the essential

06:43.600 --> 06:50.080
matrix, we can simply decompose the fundamental or the essential matrix into the camera projection

06:50.080 --> 06:52.960
matrix over here.

06:52.960 --> 06:57.760
So we can decompose this into a camera projection matrix, which means that we have obtained

06:57.760 --> 07:04.500
the motion of the camera with respect to a single frame, and this can be done using what

07:04.500 --> 07:10.360
I have mentioned earlier on here, using the relative pose estimation algorithm, or once

07:10.360 --> 07:18.240
we have the first two poses, for example, and after this we can do triangulation to

07:18.240 --> 07:19.840
get the 3D points.

07:19.840 --> 07:26.280
So in the future, subsequent views where we add in more views, we can actually simplify

07:26.280 --> 07:33.560
the 2D, 3D correspondences and apply a PMP algorithm to find the pose of this or to find

07:33.560 --> 07:38.160
the camera projection matrix of this guy over here, and that would be the absolute pose

07:38.160 --> 07:39.880
estimation algorithm.

07:39.880 --> 07:44.440
And of course, we'll use the linear triangulation algorithm that we have seen earlier on to

07:44.440 --> 07:50.680
do triangulation of the 3D points from the image correspondences and the pose of the

07:50.680 --> 07:56.040
images that we have found from the relative and absolute pose estimation algorithm.

07:56.040 --> 08:02.520
So once we have completed this procedure, the motion, computing it from the relative

08:02.520 --> 08:08.920
or absolute pose estimation algorithm, and then followed by the linear triangulation

08:08.920 --> 08:15.240
algorithm to get all the 3D points in n views, the final thing that we want to do would be

08:15.240 --> 08:21.560
the bundle adjustment algorithm, where we simply optimise over all the parameters in

08:21.560 --> 08:27.920
the camera projection matrices, over all the views, as well as the 3D points, such that

08:27.920 --> 08:36.500
the re-projected 3D points onto the image, the re-projection error of this is minimised.

08:36.500 --> 08:44.140
And as a result from this structure for motion, we'll be able to get a sparse 3D reconstruction

08:44.140 --> 08:45.620
of the scene.

08:45.620 --> 08:50.580
And next week, we'll look into more detail that the final step to this large scale 3D

08:50.580 --> 08:58.020
reconstruction pipeline would be to do a plane sweeping algorithm or a dense stereo.

08:58.020 --> 09:02.780
This is actually a dense stereo algorithm, a multi-view dense stereo algorithm, where

09:02.780 --> 09:10.780
we simply, given the pose of the camera, we simply try to get the depth of every single

09:10.780 --> 09:13.580
pixel in every image.

09:13.580 --> 09:18.460
So as a result, because we are doing this for every single pixel in every single image,

09:18.460 --> 09:23.340
so as a result, what we will get would be a dense 3D model of the scene.

09:23.340 --> 09:27.420
We'll look at this in more detail in the next lecture.

09:27.420 --> 09:31.420
Here's an illustration of the large scale 3D reconstruction pipeline.

09:31.420 --> 09:35.940
So as mentioned before, we start off with a collection of unstructured image, which

09:35.940 --> 09:39.780
we denote as I1, all the way to IN.

09:39.780 --> 09:43.120
So in the beginning here, this is a collection of unstructured image.

09:43.120 --> 09:48.720
What it means is that we do not know the relation between any of these images.

09:48.720 --> 09:55.760
They are simply treated as independent collection of images in this unstructured collection

09:55.760 --> 09:57.540
of images over here.

09:57.660 --> 10:03.020
The first step that we need to do would be to do what we call the data association.

10:03.020 --> 10:10.660
As I mentioned earlier on, we will take every pair of images in this collection of unstructured

10:10.660 --> 10:17.820
images and apply the image correspondences, extract the image key points and establish

10:17.820 --> 10:20.940
the correspondences from this pair of images.

10:20.940 --> 10:27.340
Then we will do a relative pose estimation or a robust two view estimation using the

10:27.340 --> 10:30.740
RANSAC algorithm that we have seen earlier on.

10:30.740 --> 10:36.260
From here, we will be able to establish how many inlier correspondences are there between

10:36.260 --> 10:37.260
the two images.

10:37.260 --> 10:43.100
If this set of image correspondences is more than a certain threshold, then we conclude

10:43.100 --> 10:47.740
that there is an overlapping field of view between this pair of images.

10:47.740 --> 10:53.180
As a result, what we will do would be to build what we call the scene graph over here,

10:53.180 --> 11:00.500
where if the number of inlier correspondences from the robust two view geometry exceeds

11:00.500 --> 11:05.160
a certain threshold, we will add an edge between the two images.

11:05.160 --> 11:12.300
And the nodes of this graph, the scene graph, are simply all the respective images from

11:12.300 --> 11:19.020
the collection of unstructured images here that we are given.

11:19.020 --> 11:24.140
Once we have established the scene graph, the next thing to do would be to do structure

11:24.140 --> 11:30.180
for motion and of course the first step that we want to do would be to estimate the camera

11:30.180 --> 11:39.260
poses, the camera projection matrix P1, P2, P3 of every single camera view from the collection

11:39.260 --> 11:43.060
of the images that are related in this scene graph.

11:43.060 --> 11:48.700
We do that using the relative pose estimation algorithm as well as the perspective endpoint

11:48.700 --> 11:54.380
algorithm that we have seen earlier on in the lectures.

11:54.380 --> 11:59.840
And then once we get the poses of all the cameras from the scene graph, from the pose

11:59.840 --> 12:06.380
estimation algorithm, the next thing that we can do would be to apply the linear triangulation

12:06.380 --> 12:14.780
algorithm on the image correspondences to obtain what we call the sparse 3D model over

12:14.780 --> 12:15.780
here.

12:15.780 --> 12:21.660
It's sparse because we are extracting key points, sparse sets of key points from the

12:21.660 --> 12:25.120
image to do linear triangulation.

12:25.120 --> 12:30.900
So here, in contrast to the dense modelling, we are not doing it for every single pixel,

12:30.900 --> 12:38.140
hence the number of 3D points that we will obtain would be a sparse set of 3D points.

12:38.140 --> 12:43.900
So in next week's lecture, we'll look at how to recover a dense 3D model and the example

12:43.900 --> 12:48.560
is shown here from the structure for motion pipeline.

12:48.560 --> 12:56.580
So just very briefly now is that given a set of multiple images where we know how they

12:56.580 --> 13:02.740
are related to each other, so denoted by P1, P2, the camera matrices, P3 and P4 for

13:02.740 --> 13:09.260
example, what we want to do here in this dense modelling would be to obtain the 3D depth

13:09.260 --> 13:14.800
of every single pixel in every one of these images.

13:14.800 --> 13:22.560
So as a result, we will get a dense 3D model from here.

13:22.560 --> 13:28.880
So an illustration of the structure for motion algorithm is shown in this figure over here.

13:28.880 --> 13:35.080
Suppose that we are given I1, I2 and I3 over here, three images.

13:35.080 --> 13:39.640
So the first step that we need to do would be to establish the data association that

13:39.640 --> 13:43.600
these three views are related to each other.

13:43.600 --> 13:52.720
And then the next thing would be to recover P1, P2 and P3 by doing the relative and the

13:52.720 --> 13:54.880
absolute pose estimation algorithm.

13:54.880 --> 14:00.740
And finally, from the set of sparse image correspondences, we'll do apply the linear

14:00.740 --> 14:07.120
triangulation algorithm with the known camera projection matrices to get the sparse set

14:07.120 --> 14:11.680
of 3D points as illustrated in this particular figure.

14:11.680 --> 14:17.000
And one thing to note is that all these camera poses, the camera projection matrices as well

14:17.000 --> 14:24.440
as the 3D points, they are all expressed with respect to a consistent world reference frame,

14:24.440 --> 14:28.040
which we call FW over here.

14:28.040 --> 14:31.840
Here's some examples of the structure for motion.

14:31.840 --> 14:38.600
The first example here is taken from open source pipeline, which is called COMAT.

14:38.600 --> 14:43.720
This is an open source structure for motion pipeline where you can actually input all

14:43.720 --> 14:50.320
the images and you will output something like this where each red point over here denotes

14:50.320 --> 14:56.340
a camera pose and the colored point cloud over here denotes the structure that is being

14:56.340 --> 15:00.080
reconstructed from the structure for motion pipeline.

15:00.080 --> 15:09.000
And here's an example of the city hall in San Francisco, which is done in an earlier

15:09.000 --> 15:11.040
work that I was involved in.

15:11.040 --> 15:14.640
It was published in CVPR 2016.

15:14.640 --> 15:24.800
And here's an earlier work by Samir Aragao and Noah Snavely where they reconstruct the

15:24.800 --> 15:26.520
Colosseum of Italy.

15:26.520 --> 15:33.640
You can see that every single camera image and the pose is respective pose with respect

15:33.640 --> 15:39.080
to a 3D world frame, a fixed world frame, which we call FW over here.

15:39.080 --> 15:41.400
It's illustrated in this figure over here.

15:41.400 --> 15:51.440
And the reconstructed 3D point is simply the 3D model that we want of the Colosseum.

15:51.440 --> 15:57.180
Now the first step in large scale 3D reconstruction, as we have mentioned earlier on, would be

15:57.180 --> 16:00.280
to do data association.

16:00.280 --> 16:06.080
So starting from a collection of unstructured images as illustrated in this figure over

16:06.080 --> 16:12.340
here, the final thing that we want to get would be the scene graph where the node represents

16:12.340 --> 16:16.960
every single image in the collection of images that we are given.

16:16.960 --> 16:24.560
And all the edges over here simply represents that the pair of image that is being connected

16:24.560 --> 16:30.680
by this particular edge over here has overlapping field of view.

16:30.680 --> 16:37.720
And the first step over here would be to establish what we call the connected components.

16:37.720 --> 16:43.920
So a connected component simply means that all of this, the whole set of images that's

16:43.920 --> 16:50.360
in a connected component, has overlapping field of view.

16:50.360 --> 16:55.480
And we'll make use of what we have learned earlier on in the earlier lectures, the two

16:55.480 --> 17:00.000
view geometry to establish the connected component.

17:00.000 --> 17:08.320
So we'll choose any pair of images from the collection of unstructured images and we'll

17:08.320 --> 17:16.720
first extract key points, the sift or key point correspondences, and then we'll establish

17:16.720 --> 17:23.800
the correspondences between this set of key points and followed by computing the fundamental

17:23.800 --> 17:27.320
matrix or the essential matrix in the RANSAC algorithm.

17:27.320 --> 17:32.440
And then we'll check the number of inliers to establish whether this should be a link

17:32.440 --> 17:33.480
or not.

17:33.480 --> 17:40.400
So here is the slide where I illustrate the three steps of data association, as I mentioned

17:40.400 --> 17:41.400
earlier on.

17:41.400 --> 17:47.440
Suppose that we are given a pair of images that is shown in this diagram over here, we'll

17:47.440 --> 17:52.520
first extract key points, image key points, which I will, unfortunately, I will not go

17:52.520 --> 17:59.720
too much into the detail of image key points extraction, the algorithms for sift or op.

17:59.720 --> 18:07.160
If you are interested, you should refer into these two papers, sift, which is a very famous

18:07.160 --> 18:14.480
image feature that has been used for in computer vision very extensively.

18:14.480 --> 18:21.400
And the first step over here would be to extract the image key points as well as the descriptor.

18:21.400 --> 18:25.960
So every one of this key point over here, the key point represents a single pixel or

18:25.960 --> 18:28.920
single location within the image.

18:28.920 --> 18:34.200
And every one of this single, this key point over here, it comes with a descriptor, which

18:34.200 --> 18:39.100
is usually 128 dimension for sift, for example.

18:39.100 --> 18:46.320
So this 128 dimension, you can think of it as a signature or a thumbprint for this particular

18:46.320 --> 18:48.360
location in the image.

18:48.360 --> 18:55.120
And we will use this image descriptor to match across two images.

18:55.120 --> 19:00.300
So I'm going to use this and this feature over here, this key point over here.

19:00.300 --> 19:07.100
I have two, I'm going to extract the image descriptor, the key point descriptor for this

19:07.100 --> 19:12.640
particular key point in this image, which I call i1, and then I have another image over

19:12.640 --> 19:13.960
here, i2.

19:13.960 --> 19:21.320
I'm going to extract this image descriptor, which is also a 128 dimension if I'm using

19:21.320 --> 19:24.000
sift descriptor.

19:24.000 --> 19:28.680
So I'm going to match them, a dot product, which will tell me whether they are similar

19:28.680 --> 19:30.160
to each other or not.

19:30.160 --> 19:36.360
And if they are similar, then I will say that this is going to be a match, and hence I will

19:36.360 --> 19:42.520
be able to establish these correspondences as illustrated in this particular figure over

19:42.520 --> 19:43.520
here.

19:43.520 --> 19:48.760
So a line over here simply means that I'm going to say that these two key points over

19:48.760 --> 19:51.040
here are a match.

19:51.040 --> 19:58.300
So I'm going to say that this is a punitive image correspondence in my pair of images

19:58.300 --> 19:59.300
over here.

19:59.300 --> 20:06.160
And we all know that since image correspondences are obtained purely based on appearance, that

20:06.160 --> 20:13.560
means that I do not make use of any geometrical information to establish these image correspondences

20:13.560 --> 20:16.040
up to this particular step over here.

20:16.040 --> 20:24.520
Hence we know that by only relying on the visual appearance, we are bound to get outliers

20:24.520 --> 20:29.840
matches, which means that some of these matches, for example in this case over here, they are

20:29.840 --> 20:31.720
not correct match.

20:31.720 --> 20:35.680
Visually we can tell that the correct match should be somewhere here, for example.

20:35.680 --> 20:40.920
But the sift key points that we extracted and the descriptor that we extracted gives

20:40.920 --> 20:44.120
us a wrong match over here.

20:45.040 --> 20:52.820
So the next thing that we want to do would be to apply our robust two-view geometry estimation

20:52.820 --> 20:58.800
in order to do what we call the geometric verification algorithm.

20:58.800 --> 21:05.280
So geometric verification algorithm simply means that given a putative set of correspondences,

21:05.280 --> 21:11.480
I1 and I2, I have all these correspondences, so some of them might be wrong.

21:11.480 --> 21:14.560
Some of the image correspondences might be wrong.

21:14.560 --> 21:20.320
And what I want to do here would be to apply the RANSAC starting from this set of image

21:20.320 --> 21:27.160
correspondences, which I denote as Xi and Xi'.

21:27.160 --> 21:40.120
So this set of image correspondences, and I want to run the RANSAC fundamental metric

21:40.120 --> 21:46.320
algorithm or the essential metric algorithm, depending on whether this is an uncalibrated

21:46.320 --> 21:53.880
camera or calibrated camera.

21:53.880 --> 21:59.800
So calibrated camera means that I know K, the intrinsic, and uncalibrated camera means

21:59.800 --> 22:03.440
that I do not know the intrinsic of the camera.

22:03.440 --> 22:10.960
So I can choose either one of these two-view geometry algorithms to apply RANSAC on this

22:10.960 --> 22:16.720
set of correspondences, and at the end, I will get the set of inliers based on either

22:16.720 --> 22:21.680
the fundamental metrics or the essential metrics model.

22:21.680 --> 22:28.120
And from here, we can see that after applying the algorithm, we'll be able to distinguish

22:28.120 --> 22:34.200
which are the inliers, which we illustrate colored here as the green lines, and the outlier

22:34.200 --> 22:38.000
set will be colored as the red lines over here.

22:38.000 --> 22:43.760
So once we get this, we will be able to count the total number of inliers.

22:43.760 --> 22:47.780
And if this total number of inliers exceeds a certain threshold, what this means is that

22:47.780 --> 22:55.520
there are a lot of correct image correspondences to support the two-view geometry, to support

22:55.520 --> 23:00.360
the fundamental metrics or the essential metrics that we compute.

23:00.360 --> 23:07.600
So in other words, what we are trying to do here is that we are, in addition to the establishment

23:07.600 --> 23:15.080
of the putative correspondences purely based on appearance, we are applying geometry, because

23:15.080 --> 23:18.340
this is two-view geometry that we are applying.

23:18.340 --> 23:25.200
So now we are applying geometry to further determine whether a set of image correspondences

23:25.200 --> 23:27.340
are inlier or outliers.

23:27.340 --> 23:36.560
And if there are more than a certain number of inlier counts, then we say that this fundamental

23:36.560 --> 23:39.240
metrics or the essential metrics is correct.

23:39.240 --> 23:46.000
And what it also implies is that these two views are actually seeing the same common

23:46.000 --> 23:55.040
scene where all these points are being projected onto the two views as image correspondences.

23:55.040 --> 24:00.920
And once this is determined, so if the total number of inliers exceeds a certain threshold,

24:00.920 --> 24:06.680
we will add an edge to link the two images in the scene graph.

24:06.680 --> 24:12.120
So one of the problems of doing this is that it's exhaustively searching through the pairs

24:12.120 --> 24:19.280
of images in the set of n images over here, where we are given i1 all the way to i n.

24:19.280 --> 24:26.840
So by doing this, we have to exhaustively take every pair of image from these n combinations,

24:26.840 --> 24:35.640
from all these given set of n images to establish the geometric verification, and it could become

24:35.640 --> 24:40.320
intractable when n here is too large.

24:40.320 --> 24:46.440
